<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Radner returns eight days after her first visit. The transvaginal ultrasound the next day showed solid and cystic masses in both ovaries with ascites; CT of the chest, abdomen and pelvis the day after that showed omental and peritoneal disease without spread to the liver, lungs or lymph nodes; and an ultrasound-guided core biopsy of the omentum two days later shows high-grade serous carcinoma of Mullerian origin. Dr. Varghese of gynecologic oncology saw her this morning. The bloating and early fullness are the same; she is eating small meals and has had no vomiting, and her bowels move every day. She says she is frightened but relieved to have an answer within a week. She had genetic counseling this afternoon. She has had no thoughts of self-harm. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Diagnosis</td><td>High-grade serous carcinoma of both ovaries with omental and peritoneal spread; clinical FIGO stage IIIC</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Ultrasound: bilateral complex masses, ascites. CT: omental caking, a right diaphragm nodule, no liver, lung or nodal disease</td></tr><tr><td>Symptoms</td><td>Bloating and early fullness unchanged; no vomiting or bowel obstruction</td></tr><tr><td>Coordination</td><td>Gynecologic oncology seen today; surgery planned in one week; genetic counseling done</td></tr><tr><td>Her questions</td><td>Whether surgery can remove it all, and what the genetic test means for her family</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

