    <title>Problems</title>
    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Persistent bloating with tightness of her clothes, feeling full after a few bites, a dull lower abdominal and pelvic ache, and passing urine more often, on most days for about four weeks, in a woman of 39: the ovarian cancer symptom index is positive. Examination: a distended abdomen with shifting dullness and a firm, fixed, irregular mass in the right adnexa. Suspected ovarian cancer; transvaginal ultrasound, CT, CA-125 and gynecologic oncology review booked within the week</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R19.00</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting problem linked to her documented abdominal symptoms; superseded by the diagnosis at the gynecologic oncology consultation.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>High-grade serous carcinoma of both ovaries (Mullerian origin), diagnosed by image-guided omental core biopsy (PAX8 and WT1 positive, p53 aberrant), with bilateral adnexal masses, ascites, omental and diaphragmatic disease on CT: clinical FIGO stage IIIC; synthetic modern detail linked to her documented ovarian cancer</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>C56.3</td><td>S — Synthetic histology and stage; the documented diagnosis was stage IV ovarian cancer (D).</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Peritoneal and omental metastases of the ovarian cancer, with ascites</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>C78.6</td><td>S — Synthetic extent of disease on CT and biopsy.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-4">
    <td>Family history of ovarian cancer in three relatives (her grandmother, an aunt, and a cousin) in a woman of Ashkenazi Jewish ancestry; no genetic testing in the family is known</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>Z80.41</td><td>S — Synthetic coding of her documented family history; the basis for genetic counseling.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

